a systematic review [cited 28 April 2020] PLoSOne. 2012;7(4) doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0035797. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA). Infectious Diseases Society of America Guidelines on Infection Prevention for Health Care Personnel Caring for Patients with Suspected or Known COVID-19 [Accessed 28 Abr 2020]. Available from: https://www.idsociety.org/news--publications-new/articles/2020/idsa-releases-covid-19-infection-prevention-guidelines-for-health-care-personnel/. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 32. Dudreuilh C., Kumar N., Moxham V., Hemsley C., Goldenberg S., Moutzouris D.A. De-isolation of COVID- positive haemodialysis patients in the outpatient setting: a single centre experience. Kidney Int. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.kint.2020.04.021. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Lai X.L., Wang H.Y., Guo Z.Y. Recommendations for prevention and management of COVID-19 in peritoneal dialysis patients. Chronic Dis Transl Med. 2020;6:115–118. doi: 10.1016/j.cdtm.2020.04.003. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Parisien R.L., Shin M., Constant M., Saltzman B.M., Li X., Levine W.N., et al. Telehealth Utilization in Response to the Novel Coronavirus (COVID-19) Pandemic in Orthopaedic Surgery. J Am Acad Orthop Surg. 2020;28:E487–E492. doi: 10.5435/JAAOS-D-20-00339. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Smith W.R., Atala A.J., Terlecki R.P., Kelly E.E., Matthews C.A. Implementation Guide for Rapid Integration of an Outpatient Telemedicine Program during the COVID-19 Pandemic. J Am Coll Surg. 2020 doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2020.04.030. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Li Y., Yang N., Li X., Wang J., Yan T. Strategies for Prevention and Control of the 2019 Novel Coronavirus Disease in the Department of Kidney Transplantation. Transpl Int. 2020 doi: 10.1111/tri.13634. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Kang L., Ma S., Chen M., Yang J., Wang Y., Li R., et al. Impact on Mental Health and Perceptions of Psychological Care among Medical and Nursing Staff in Wuhan during the 2019 Novel Coronavirus Disease Outbreak: a Cross-sectional Study. Brain Behav Immun. 2020;87:11–17. doi: 10.1016/j.bbi.2020.03.028. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Schwartz J., King C.C., Yen M.Y. Protecting Health Care Workers during the COVID-19 Coronavirus Outbreak -Lessons from Taiwan’s SARS response. Clin Infect Dis. 2020 doi: 10.1093/cid/ciaa255. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',45,'2025-10-15 06:34:42','2025-10-15 06:34:42');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3422,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12278578','Predictors of failure to transition from pediatric to adult nephrology care.','Nishi L, Kang J, Ghossein C.','2025','lch lch discontinuity devalued regrettably excepted lch lch ethinicity functinon anomolies abstractly nonattendance unattended protocolized systematized downtown preemptively egfrs philanthropy philanthropy authorizing blubaum frimberger slobodov atger lallier guilmin crepon boizeau mistry blyderveen punthakee pankhurst evison mytton kerecuk lipkin gfaa059 roosevelt brandon yetman bitencourt bermas solow makris lotstein seid claeys hoebeke jpurol overbury bohnsack frech hersh stam nijhof monninkhof heymans grootenhuis jadohealth raygoza cortez suris akre jadohealth dantas cyrino hamacher gipson saydah imperatore pavkov nishi ghossein xkme somerville viner cybulsky cercena goodyer suri kamphuis pelt vroed hazes jmw soren pedn bryce atherton bjgp skelton halvorson jhq dicianno javalkar socioecologic e117 pcd11 jaleel andemariam owarish boruchov thrall hagstrom resmini rawlinson wagoner jpepsy jsx142 bartholomew rig','Abstract Background The increased prevalence and life expectancy of young adults with chronic kidney disease has led to a greater need to transition patients from pediatric to adult nephrology care. This care transition occurs at a highly vulnerable time in life, and rates of unsuccessful transition are high. Evidence-based transition protocols can greatly facilitate the process. Accordingly, Northwestern Medicine (NM) partnered with Lurie Children’s Hospital (LCH) in 2014 to develop a nephrology transition clinic to help support these high-risk patients. To best allocate resources, it is important to identify those young adults most vulnerable to drop out. Here we report on the success of our clinic and factors affecting failure to transition. Methods This was a retrospective chart review of patients seen in the NM/LCH transition clinic from 2014 to 2022. Demographic and clinical data of diagnosis, zip code, distance from clinic, average household income, parental/caregiver presence, working/school status, no-show rate, insurance, and baseline kidney function were collected. For those who successfully transitioned, data on prompt communications were also collected. Results Overall, 92% of patients successfully transitioned to adult care. 27% of patients missed their first adult center appointment and required execution of a prompt protocol. After this intervention, an additional 19% of patients successfully transitioned. 39% of those who failed to transition had a primary diagnosis of hypertension as compared to only 9% in those who transitioned successfully. Those who successfully transitioned had a lower mean estimated glomerular filtration rate of 89 mL/min/1.73 m² as compared to 116 mL/min/1.73 m² in the group who failed to transition. 15% of those who failed to transition lacked insurance as compared to only 1% of patients who successfully transitioned. Finally, those who required prompts had a significantly higher no-show rate at the pediatric center at 10% as compared to 6% in those not requiring prompts. Conclusion Transition clinics can help promote the successful transition from pediatric to adult nephrology care. Higher kidney function, lack of insurance, and primary diagnosis of hypertension were significantly correlated with failure to transition, and a higher pediatric center no-show rate was significantly correlated with the need for prompts in this single-center study. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Keywords: Chronic kidney disease, Pediatric nephrology, Transition of care, Risk factors, Transition clinic Background Young adults with chronic disease exit the pediatric health care system and enter adult care during a highly vulnerable time in life. Unfortunately, they may fail to engage in the adult medical system and experience gaps in care [ 1 ]. Rates of unsuccessful health care transition are between 3 and 49% across a spectrum of chronic disease [ 2 – 5 ]. The discontinuity of care during this time is associated with increased morbidity and mortality, including adverse outcomes of increased hospitalization, increased surgical intervention, poor diabetic control, and renal allograft loss [ 6 – 10 ]. Contrasting models of care and disparate resource availability between pediatric and adult medical centers contribute to the challenges of transition. As compared to pediatric practices, adult care centers often lack the supportive services for optimal care coordination, with limited multidisciplinary staffing, less ancillary services, and inadequate financial resources (such as those received through state Title V) [ 11 , 12 ]. Moreover, while pediatric care models are family-centric, with an emphasis on the parent/caregiver involvement in medical plan adherence, adult care models value independence and self-direction, with the family role often being devalued [ 11 – 13 ]. Regrettably for the young adult population, independence is an underdeveloped skill at this time in life. To address these obstacles and facilitate the transition from pediatric to adult care, there is a call for evidence-based transition approaches, including the establishment of formal transition clinics [ 1 , 14 ]. Structured transition interventions initiated during adolescence and spanning into adulthood have been shown to reduce barriers to transition, increase satisfaction with care, improve medication adherence, and improve outcomes such as lowering the decline in renal function [ 15 – 17 ]. While there is no universally excepted definition of successful transition, there is consensus that patients attending their first appointment and not missing visits at the adult medical center is essential [ 18 ]. While there is little data in the transition population, factors affecting missed appointments in the general population have been shown to be time between visits, insurance, socioeconomic status, distance to clinic, and prior no-show rate [ 19 , 20 ]. The prevalence and life expectancy of adolescents with chronic kidney disease (CKD) has steadily increased, thereby escalating the number of patients who require transition from pediatric to adult nephrology care [ 21 , 22 ]. Northwestern Medicine (NM) partnered with Lurie Children’s Hospital (LCH) in 2014 to establish a nephrology transition clinic [ 23 ]. Understanding predictors of failure to transition are imperative for pediatric and adult teams to effectively allocate support to those at highest risk for drop out. In this study, we aimed to describe the success rate of our transition clinic and identify factors associated with failure to transition. Methods This study was a retrospective chart review that included all patients seen in the NM Lurie Nephrology transition clinic from May 2014 to December 2022 [ 23 ]. Both are large academic medical centers in the city of Chicago serving a diverse patient population. All patients were aged 18 and over. Patients with kidney transplants were seen in the NM Lurie transition clinic starting in 2022. The pediatric team identified patients ready to transition and patients were scheduled with the adult care team in the transition clinic. The adult multidisciplinary team consisted of a nephrologist, physician associate, and social worker. Each provider saw the patient individually at the transfer visit. The pediatric and adult teams communicated prior to the office visit to review medical and psychosocial issues. Potential barriers to transition and medical history were discussed. The transfer visit occurred at LCH and subsequent visits occurred at NM. The two clinics are located across the street from one another connected by an indoor bridge. Patients were provided with information on the adult care location and were discharged with a future appointment with the adult providers. Patients were asked about barriers and concerns about transition and questions were addressed. Patients who deemed NM clinic to be too far away or difficult to get to were provided with an alternative adult nephrologist closer to home. If the patient no longer required nephrology follow up, he/she was discharged from clinic and the team subsequently confirmed that the patient followed up with their primary care provider or other specialist. Successful transition was defined as one documented visit at the adult care center and unsuccessful transition was an absence of a visit for 1.5 years after the transfer visit. All patients received a standard automated call or text reminder of their appointment. A prompt protocol (additional phone calls, emails, texts and electronic medical record reminders) was initiated for any missed visits. Patients were contacted at regular intervals until they could be reached. This study received IRB approval from Northwestern University IRB and Lurie Children’s Hospital IRB. Data collection Baseline demographic and clinical data including sex at birth, race/ethnicity (self-reported), nephrology diagnosis, zip code, age, distance from clinic, average household income, parental/caregiver presence at transfer visit, working/in school, insurance, and baseline kidney function were collected retrospectively from the pediatric and adult medical record (EMR). US Census Bureau data was used to determine average household income based on zip code. Distance from clinic was computed by using Google Maps and ChatGPT from household zip code to medical center zip code. No-show rates for all visits (including primary and specialty care) to the pediatric center were collected from the pediatric EMR. Kidney function was calculated using the CKD-EPI Creatinine and CKD-EPI Creatinine-Cystatin C 2021 formulas. All prompt communications that were recorded in the EMR were collected. Calls regarding issues unrelated to missed visits were not included in the analysis. Results are reported as mean and standard deviation for normally distributed variables, percent (%) for categorical variables and median with interquartile range for non-normally distributed variables. The R program version 4.2.1 and Excel were used to execute statistical analysis. Statistical significance was considered to be at a null hypothesis P < .05. Results Over an 8-year period from 2014 to 2022, 166 patients were seen in the nephrology transition clinic. Of these, 154 patients were eligible to transition to adult care and were included in the analysis. Five patients were discharged from nephrology care at the transfer visit and 7 were transferred to external nephrologists. The most common diagnoses were glomerular disease (28%) and congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract (27%). 73% of patients successfully transitioned initially. 27% of patients missed their follow up appointment and a prompt protocol was executed. An additional 19% of patients successfully transitioned after escalated communication. In total, 92% of patients ultimately successfully transitioned to adult care. Characteristics of patients seen in the transition clinic are summarized in Table 1 . Hypertension (HTN) was the primary diagnosis in 39% of those who failed to transition as compared to only 9% in those who successfully transitioned ( P = .02). The other diagnoses were similar between the groups. Findings were similar between the groups with respect to race, ethnicity, household income, and distance from clinic. Most of the patients who did not successfully transition were male, but this was not statistically significant. Age of disease onset was similar at 6–10 years. Most patients came with a parent/caregiver and were in school at the time of the visit. The group that successfully transitioned had a significantly lower mean estimated glomerular filtration rate of 89 mL/min/1.73 m² as compared to 116 mL/min/1.73 m² ( P = .02) in those lost to follow. Finally, 15% of those who failed to transition did not have insurance as compared to only 1% of patients who successfully transitioned ( P = .01). Table 1. Patient baseline characteristics according to transition success Characteristic Successful transition (n=141) Unsuccessful transition (n=13) P Value Demographics Gender, n (%) Female 70 (50) 3 (23) 0.12 Male 71 (50) 10 (77) Race and ethnicity, n (%) Non-hispanic white 70 (50) 6 (46) 0.15 Hispanic 56 (40) 5 (39) Asian 8 (5) 0 (0) Non-hispanic black 7 (5) 2 (15) Age, year, mean (SD) 20 (2) 20 (1) 0.48 Clinical Factors Diagnosis, n (%) Glomerulonephritis 40 (28) 3 (23) 0.02 CAKUT 38 (27) 3 (23) Genetic disorders 23 (16) 1 (8) Hypertension 12 (9) 5 (39) Non-kidney organ transplant 11 (8) 0 (0) Chemotherapy/Medication related 6 (4) 0 (0) Kidney transplant 2 (1) 1 (8) Other 9 (6) 0 (0) Age of disease onset, year, mean (SD) 6 (7) 10 (8) 0.16 Baseline kidney function, mL/min/1.73 m 2 CKD-EPI Creatinine-Cystatin C Mean (SD) 89 (36) 116 (15) 0.02 Median [Min, Max] 97 [10, 148] 122 [83, 128] CKD-EPI Creatinine Mean (SD) 97 (37) 119 (32) 0.04 Median [Min, Max] 104 [3,147] 131 [17, 141] Non-Clinical Factors Distance to clinic, miles, mean (SD) 29 (63) 21 (21) 0.31 Household income, dollars, mean (SD) 71200 (25700) 61400 (21400) 0.14 Pediatric no show rate, mean (SD) 7(6) 11 (11) 0.28 Work status, n (%) Working 52 (37) 4 (31) 0.89 Not working 89 (63) 9 (69) School status, n (%) In school 94 (67) 9 (69) 1.00 Not in school 46 (33) 4 (31) Parent/Caregiver presence, n (%) Present 102 (72) 7 (54) 0.17 Not present 32 (23) 6 (46) Unknown 7 (5) 0 (0) Insurance, n (%) Private 82 (58) 5 (39) 0.01 Government 58 (41) 6 (46) No insurance 1 (1) 2 (15) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CAKUT, congenital anomalies of kidney and urinary tract; eGFR, estimated glomerular filtration rate; SD, standard deviation The characteristics of those who successfully transitioned according to prompt protocol are shown in Table 2 . There was no significant difference between the groups with respect to primary diagnosis, race, ethnicity, average household income, and distance from clinic. There was a small but significant difference between the groups in pediatric center no-show rate, with a no-show rate of 10% in those requiring a prompt as compared to 6% in those who did not ( P = .04). Table 2. Baseline characteristics according to prompt status Characteristics No prompt (n = 112) Prompt (n = 29) P value Demographics Gender, n (%) Female 56 (50) 14 (48) 1.00 Male 56 (50) 15 (52) Ethinicity/Race, n (%) Non-hispanic white 59 (53) 11 (38) 0.23 Hispanic 40 (36) 16 (56) Asian 7 (6) 1 (3) Non-hispanic black 6 (5) 1 (3) Age, year, n (%) 20 (2) 20 (2) 0.83 Clinical Factors Diagnosis, n (%) Glomerulonephritis 32 (29) 8 (28) 0.99 CAKUT 28 (25) 10 (34) Genetic disorders 20 (18) 3 (10) Hypertension 10 (9) 2 (7) Non-kidney organ transplant 9 (8) 2 (7) Chemotherapy/medication induced 4 (4) 2 (7) Kidney transplant 2 (1) 0 (0) Other 7 (6) 2 (7) Age of disease onset, years, mean (SD) 6 (7) 7 (7) 0.89 Baseline kidney functinon, mL/min/1.73 m 2 CKD-EPI Creatinine-Cystatin C Mean (SD) 89 (36) 86 (32) 0.72 Median [Min, Max] 97 [10, 148] 96 [18, 136] CKD-EPI Creatinine Mean (SD) 94 (38) 98 (37) 0.61 Median [Min, Max] 96 [18, 147] 106 [3, 144] Non-Clinical Factors Distance from clinic, miles, mean (SD) 25 (25) 45 (130) 0.41 Household income, dollars, mean (SD) 73000 (25100) 64300 (27500) 0.13 Pediatric no show rate, mean (SD) 6 (5) 10 (9) 0.04 Work status, n (%) Working 40 (36) 12 (41) 0.73 Not working 72 (64) 17 (59) School status, n (%) In school 78 (70) 16 (55) 0.19 Not In school 33 (30) 13 (45) Parent/Caregiver presence, n (%) Present 83 (74) 19 (66) 0.30 Not present 23 (21) 9 (31) Unknown 6 (5) 1 (3) Insurance, n (%) Private 65 (58) 17 (59) 0.88 Government 46 (41) 12 (41) None 1 (1) 0 (0) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CAKUT, congenital anomolies of kidney and urinary tract; eGFR, estimated glomerular filtration rate; SD, standard deviation Discussion Pediatric to adult health care transition is a time of great vulnerability. Factors contributing to care disruption involve systemic differences between adult and pediatric care centers and challenges inherent to human psychosocial maturation. While the start of legal adulthood is 18 years old, neurocognitive development continues until the late 20’s to early 30’s [ 24 ]. Young adults are still developing the ability to think abstractly, a skill which allows one to envision the future. The connection between medical appointment nonattendance to future health outcomes is still an abstraction in the young adult mind [ 25 , 26 ]. Over and above the normal timeline of human maturation, young adults with CKD and other chronic disease may have a more pronounced impairment in executive function, reduced autonomy, and slowed identify development as compared to healthy peers [ 13 , 26 , 27 ]. Nephrology transition clinics can help support the successful transition of young adults with kidney disease [ 1 , 14 ]. In this study, we found that 27% of patients missed their initial adult nephrology clinic appointment. We were able to capture an additional 19% of patients through prompts. It has been shown that young adults with chronic illness who had a year or more of unattended visits can still be captured for follow up and protocolized reminders can help bolster transition success [ 4 , 28 ]. Overall, our 92% successful transition rate is similar to those seen in other chronic disease clinics and supports the recommendation for systematized transition clinics [ 1 , 4 , 12 , 29 , 30 ]. Those who required a prompt had a statistically higher no-show rate at the pediatric institution. This no-show rate is somewhat crude as it encompasses all visits to the pediatric site and did not account for nuanced reasons for missed visits. Nevertheless, several studies have identified previously missed visits as a risk factor for no-shows [ 19 , 31 , 32 ]. Our data suggests that a higher pediatric no-show rate may help predict those patients who may need enhanced communication strategies to ensure appropriate follow up. Work supported by The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Disease identified insurance coverage as a risk factor for being lost to follow up [ 33 ]. 15% of our patients who did not transition lacked insurance, which is in accordance with general population data showing a higher rate of no shows in patients who are uninsured [ 19 , 31 – 33 ]. While our study did not show a difference between patients with private and public health insurance, it has been shown that CKD patients with private insurance had higher rates of health care utilization and greater self-care skills as compared to those with public insurance [ 34 ]. Overall in youths with chronic disease, those with insurance have shown an increased readiness to transition as compared to those who lack insurance [ 35 ]. Given these findings, transition clinics should proactively identify/predict insurance lapses and provide resources to ensure ongoing financial coverage. Although travel distance to clinic is not a consistent risk factor for missed follow up in the general adult or pediatric population, it remains an important consideration where young adult patients have not yet established with their health care provider [ 19 , 36 , 37 ]. Our clinic is centrally located in downtown Chicago and is accessible by car and public transportation. Most importantly, it is across the street from the pediatric hospital where our patients previously received long-term care. Our results showed that distance from clinic was not predictive of a successful transition. Transportation, however, was intentionally addressed at the transfer visit and if a patient felt that clinic location was a barrier to transition, arrangements were made to see a local nephrologist. These patients were discharged from clinic and not included in the analysis. We postulate that this group would have been at higher risk for failure to transition had these alternative arrangements not been made. General population studies have found a higher no-show rate in those with lower socioeconomic and sociodemographic status [ 1 , 3 , 19 , 31 , 32 , 38 ]. Additionally, some studies show that ethnic and cultural pressures are factors associated with missed appointments in the young adult population [ 31 , 39 ]. In our study, we did not find a significant difference in ethnicity, race, or average household income between the groups. We hypothesize that the structured transition protocol, with a heavy reliance on social work assessment of socioeconomic barriers, helped to preemptively address many of these issues. Patients who successfully transitioned had a lower baseline mean kidney function than those who were lost to follow up. These findings suggest that the patients who were “healthier” were more likely to miss their appointments. 39% of those who did not successfully transition had a primary diagnosis of HTN, a typically asymptomatic disease. It may be that these patients felt well, did not appreciate the health implications of HTN, and/or were unaware of the importance of follow up. Patients/caregivers who perceive themselves to be in good health, have less complex medical problems, and less medication use have been shown to have higher no-show rates that those with greater disease burden [ 20 , 32 , 36 , 37 ]. Based on these findings, emphasis should be placed on patients with normal/higher eGFRs who feel in good health, and protocols should incorporate education on the importance of follow up into adulthood. This study has several limitations. This was a single-center, retrospective chart review and results may not be generalizable. Data such as race and ethnicity were gathered from the EMR and can introduce self-reported bias. Income data is limited in that estimates were based on zip code alone. As there is no universally accepted definition of successful transition, the study’s external validity is limited. Further research is needed to determine how best to define successful transition. Finally, this study was conducted within a well-established transition clinic with dedicated resources (social work, ancillary staff, philanthropy), and our data may not relate to young adults transitioning outside of this highly structured context (for example in a general nephrology clinic). Conclusion Transition clinics are important to successfully transition young adults with chronic kidney disease from pediatric to adult care. Most patients can successfully transition their health care with the right resources. In our study, higher kidney function, lack of insurance, and primary diagnosis of HTN correlated with failure to transition, while a higher pediatric center no-show rate correlated with the need for prompts. These factors may help stratify those at greatest risk in this highly vulnerable cohort. More studies are needed to further validate these high-risk factors so that transition teams can best allocate resources. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions The authors confirm contribution to the paper as follows: study conception and design: L.N., C.G.; data collection: J.K., L.N.; analysis and interpretation of results: L.N., C.G.; draft manuscript preparation: L.N, C.G. All authors reviewed the results and approved the final version of the manuscript. Funding Funding for research data set report was received through gift through NM Philanthropy for NM Lurie Transition Clinic. Data availability The datasets generated and/or analyzed during the current study are not publicly available given the presence of rare diseases and the possibility that data could be linked to a subject, but some are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate In accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, this study was reviewed and approved by Northwestern University IRB and Lurie Children’s Hospital IRB. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. White HW, Cooley WC, Transitions Clinical Report Authorizing Group, American Academy of Pediatrics, American Academy of Family Physicians. American College of Physicians. Supporting the health care transition from adolescence to adulthood in the medical home. Pediatrics. 2018;142(5). 10.1542/peds.2018-2587 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 2. Blubaum A, Lewis J, Frimberger D, Slobodov G. 9-year evaluation of a transitional care program for congenital neurogenic bladder patients. Urology. 2023;180:285–90. 10.1016/j.urology.2023.06.023 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Atger-Lallier L, Guilmin-Crepon S, Boizeau P, et al. Factors affecting loss to follow-up in children and adolescents with chronic endocrine conditions. Horm Res Paediatr. 2019;92(4):254–61. 10.1159/000505517 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Mistry B, Van Blyderveen S, Punthakee Z, Grant C. Condition-related predictors of successful transition from paediatric to adult care among adolescents with type 1 diabetes. Diabet Med. 2015;32(7):881–5. 10.1111/dme.12746 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Hart LC, Pollock M, Brown A, et al. Where did they go? Tracking young adult follow-up during the transition from pediatric to adult-oriented care. Clin Pediatr. 2019;58(11–12):1277–83. 10.1177/0009922819852980 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Pankhurst T, Evison F, Mytton J, Williamson S, Kerecuk L, Lipkin G. Young adults have worse kidney transplant outcomes than other age groups. Nephrol Dial Transplant. 2020;35(6):1043–1051. 10.1093/ndt/gfaa059 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 7. Foster BJ. Heightened graft failure risk during emerging adulthood and transition to adult care. Pediatr Nephrol. 2015;30(4):567–76. 10.1007/s00467-014-2859-7 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 8. Yeung E, Kay J, 