INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3448,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7780545','Expanding opportunities and emerging challenges: broadening the scope of genetic testing in nephrology.','Groopman EE, Gharavi AG.','2019','connaughton spectacular multigene nephropathies connaughton connaughton nonfamilial nondiagnostic col4a3 col4a4 col4a connaughton connaughton nephropathies nephropathies connaughton connaughton explicative nephropathies nphs2 r229q col4a5 g624d indiscriminate connaughton nephropathies explicative nephrologic putatively connaughton phenotyped multigene nondiagnostic nephrologic nephrologic bonafide putatively hg008680 aag f30 dk116473 vivante devuyst knoers connaughton shril lata marasa rasouly groopman kantor col4a3 col4a4 posey harel exomes','Abstract Massively parallel sequencing technologies such as exome sequencing are increasingly applied across medicine. Connaughton et al. report a high diagnostic yield of exome sequencing among adults with hereditary nephropathy or nephropathy of unknown cause. Their findings support broader use of genomic sequencing in nephrology and highlight key associated questions, including how to identify those patients for whom testing is indicated, pinpoint pathogenic variants, and balance the resultant health care benefits and clinical follow-up burden. In recent years, massively parallel sequencing has enabled high-throughput assessment of individual human genomes for causal variants for a variety of clinical disorders, supporting specific diagnosis and, in many cases, guiding management. In adult medicine, these advances have been most spectacular in oncology, an area in which genome-wide sequence analysis of tumor and germline DNA have enabled selection of therapies targeted to the underlying molecular defects. To date, applications of massively parallel sequencing in nephrology have been limited largely to patients with pediatric-onset and/or familial disease, with use of multigene panels targeting solely the clinically suspected nephropathy. 1 However, the fact that Mendelian causes currently are thought to account for approximately 10% of cases of end-stage kidney failure, and Mendelian nephropathies show high genetic and phenotypic heterogeneity, 2 provides strong rationale for expanding both the scope of genes and clinical indications tested. In this issue of Kidney International , Connaughton et al. 3 contribute significantly to a growing body of work demonstrating the value and challenges of performing such expanded genetic testing in nephrology. 4 , 5 Connaughton et al. 3 evaluated the diagnostic yield of exome sequencing (ES) in a cohort of 114 Irish adult probands with chronic kidney disease (CKD) of diverse etiologies, with nonfamilial as well as familial disease. In ES, the coding portion of the genome is captured and sequenced, enabling assessment of the majority of human genes, including the ~3600 already known to be associated with Mendelian disorders. This technique thus substantially expands the diagnostic search space as compared to phenotype-driven panels, which typically feature several dozen genes. Thus, ES eliminates the need for sequential gene panel testing for patients with nondiagnostic CKD—etiology unknown, and can clarify the clinical impression for those who may have been initially misdiagnosed. The genome-wide approach of ES also has uncovered genotype–phenotype relationships that were not previously appreciated. For example, ES studies have demonstrated that COL4A3 and COL4A4 mutations can manifest as focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, expanding the phenotypic spectrum of COL4A -associated nephropathy. 6 Finally, use of ES has revealed that a subset of patients harbor diagnostic mutations for 2 distinct Mendelian disorders, 7 helping clinicians to resolve complex clinical presentations. The patients assessed by Connaughton et al. 3 span the major categories of hereditary nephropathy, such as cystic kidney disease, congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract, steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome, and tubulointerstitial disease, as well as CKD—etiology unknown. Of these 114 probands, 94 (81%) harbored other features consistent with a hereditary form of disease, with 78 (68%) reporting a positive family history of CKD and 16 (14%) having extra-renal features. Connaughton et al. 3 report an overall diagnostic yield of 37% (42 of 114), encompassing 29 distinct single-gene etiologies of CKD. Of these 29 monogenic disorders, 21 (72%) were detected only once, in a single proband, highlighting the genetic heterogeneity of Mendelian nephropathies. Among the 42 positive cases, ES reclassified the clinically suspected disease in 9 (22%), and for 16 (38%), it provided a diagnosis for CKD—etiology unknown. Diagnostic yield was higher among cases with other clinical features supportive of a Mendelian etiology of nephropathy, with detection rates of 69% (11 of 16) and 36% (28 of 78) among individuals with a positive family history for CKD and extra-renal features, respectively, versus 15% (3 of 20) in those without either, supporting them as indications for genetic testing. However, yield did not significantly differ between cases with versus without early-onset disease (prior to age 18 years), or according to median age of ESRD. Finally, consistent with prior reports, 7 dual diagnoses were detected in 4.7% of positive cases, who harbored diagnostic mutations in 2 different Mendelian nephropathy-associated genes. Altogether, these findings provide valuable insight regarding the diagnostic yield of ES and associated genetic and phenotypic spectrum among patients clinically diagnosed with various hereditary nephropathies. The high detection rate, particularly among patients with CKD—etiology unknown, supports prior reports of the merits of more-comprehensive genetic analysis, helping end the “diagnostic odyssey” experienced by patients and their clinicians. On case-level evaluation, Connaughton et al. 3 find that in many cases, the genetic findings have important implications for clinical management, including reclassification of disease, initiation of targeted workup, and changes in therapy. The clinical implications of their findings are remarkably concordant with another recently published study of ES in adults with CKD, 4 highlighting the promise of genetic diagnostics to support targeted interventions, and if performed early enough, even presymptomatic disease screening and preventive care. Connaughton et al. 3 perform a targeted analysis of ES data, filtering for rare (defined here using control minor allele frequency cutoffs of 0.1% for dominant disorders and 1% for recessive disorders) protein- or splice-altering variants occurring in a set of 478 Mendelian nephropathy-associated genes. The curated gene list represents a valuable contribution to ongoing efforts to comprehensively catalog and characterize genes associated with Mendelian nephropathy, 2 , 4 , 5 and the phenotype-driven approach supports efficient identification of variants explicative for the primary renal disease indication. The frequency-based filtering applied has been demonstrated to greatly reduce the burden of misclassified variants emerging from ES, 5 reflecting the fact that the majority of causal variants for Mendelian disorders are ultra-rare. However, this approach holds the challenge of determining the appropriate minor allele frequency thresholds. Certain established causal variants for Mendelian nephropathies, such as the NPHS2 p.R229Q allele, associated with steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome, and the COL4A5 p.G624D allele, associated with X-linked Alport syndrome, are present at appreciable control frequencies, such that they could be removed with indiscriminate use of ultra-stringent minor allele frequency–-based filtering. 5 As in prior diagnostic massively parallel sequencing studies in nephrology, 4 , 8 Connaughton et al. 3 surveyed a selected population comprised of patients harboring clinical diagnoses consistent with inherited forms of renal disease, 78% of whom also had a positive family history for nephropathy. In contrast, most individuals with CKD are clinically diagnosed with acquired causes, such as diabetes- and hypertension-attributed nephropathy, and across etiologies, approximately 25% of cases report a positive family history. Moreover, 99% of the cases assessed in this study self-identified as white Europeans, further limiting its generalizability to the greater, ethnically diverse CKD population. The yield of genetic testing can vary widely among clinical categories of renal disease, 8 as can the frequencies of candidate pathogenic variants by ethnicity. Thus, assessment of an ethnically diverse cohort including patients representing all major etiologic categories is needed to provide insight regarding the genetic and phenotypic spectrum of Mendelian nephropathies, resolve the clinical relevance of more common alleles, and ultimately develop evidence-based guidelines for genetic testing in nephrology. These findings also point to future challenges for nephrologists. Should ES be deployed as a predictive test, as may occur with broader use of genetic testing across clinical medicine? Generally, patients undergoing clinical ES receive variants classified as pathogenic, likely pathogenic, or of uncertain significance in genes related to the primary disease indication for testing; however, many laboratories now offer the option of also receiving otherwise medically relevant genetic findings unrelated to this primary disease indication, including those for other Mendelian disorders. 9 Both how to interpret a candidate pathogenic variant for nephropathy emerging from diagnostic ES that is also found in adults not known to have CKD and how rare a variant must be in the general population to be causal are unclear at present, contributing to the high burden of variants of uncertain significance. Conversely, the genome-wide scope of ES also can result in the discovery of medically actionable mutations for Mendelian disorders other than nephropathy. Such secondary findings represent both an advantage and challenge of genomic sequencing, because although they are not explicative for a patient’s CKD, they not only can initiate extra-nephrologic subspecialty referrals, but also may have meaningful implications for its management. For example, secondary findings for hereditary cancer predisposition could not only trigger oncologic workup, but also inform usage of immunosuppressive therapy, in the context of the patient’s primary renal disease and/or renal transplantation. Given the abundance of rare, putatively damaging variation present in each human genome, and the fact that many Mendelian disorders have multi-organ involvement, broader usage of genomic sequencing could lead to patients and their families undergoing considerable clinical workup involving multiple subspecialty referrals and substantial health care spending. 5 Thus, how to balance the opportunity for early detection of medically actionable conditions with the potential financial and psychosocial burden of the associated medical follow-up remains unclear. Massively parallel sequencing–based genetic testing promises to revolutionize the workup of suspected hereditary nephropathy, enabling specific diagnosis and personalized care. The findings of Connaughton et al. 3 not only support this promise, but also point to key gaps in our knowledge that must be filled to realize it ( Figure 1 ). Future studies that assess the frequency of pathogenic variants for Mendelian nephropathy in well-phenotyped cases and controls, compare the diagnostic yield of ES across broader clinical indications and relative to targeted panels, and examine the long-term health benefits and costs of genetic testing, will provide much-needed insight regarding these questions, and help to achieve genomic medicine for patients with kidney disease. Figure 1 |. Massively parallel sequencing (MPS) in nephrology: current practices, potential future usage, and associated questions. Open in a new tab The key aspects of MPS in nephrology include (i) patient populations assessed and determination of the first-line genetic testing modality; (ii) genetic analysis procedures; and (iii) clinical application of the genetic findings detected. ( a ) Current use of MPS consists largely of applying disease-targeted multigene panels as a first-line test to probands clinically diagnosed with the associated disease. If the clinical impression is uncertain or the presentation wholly nondiagnostic, sequential gene panel testing or exome sequencing (ES) or genome sequencing (GS) may be applied instead. Genetic analysis assesses rare variants occurring in the genes included on the panel; for ES or GS, rare variants in known Mendelian nephropathy-associated genes are assessed. Return of results and follow-up clinical care are generally conducted as part of the patient’s routine nephrologic care, with his or her existing nephrologist. ( b ) Potential future use of MPS involves applying genome-wide testing (exome sequencing or genome sequencing) to a broader population, which may include individuals not known to have chronic kidney disease (CKD; e.g., for purposes of disease screening and presymptomatic genetic diagnosis, or, among probands undergoing genomic sequencing for other disease indications, as secondary genetic findings) as well as patients with CKD of diverse etiologies, including those presumed to have acquired causes of disease (e.g., diabetic nephropathy). The scope of genetic analysis may expand to all genes (genome-wide), integrating genetic diagnostics with case-control analyses for novel gene discovery. As the disorders detected often involve multiple organ systems, and the patients sequenced may not have previously been under nephrologic care, return of results and clinical follow-up will involve a multidisciplinary team, including other medical subspecialists for the organ systems affected by the given disease, and genetic counselors. ( c ) The key questions associated with this expanded, future use include (i) the clinical indications for genetic workup for Mendelian forms of CKD, (ii) determining which genes should be assessed and which filtering parameters most effectively identify bonafide disease-causal variants in the context of the abundance of putatively damaging variation present in any human genome, and (iii) determining which health care professionals should be involved and how to balance the opportunity to identify potentially medically actionable conditions with the financial and psychosocial burdens of the often considerable clinical follow-up. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This work is supported by grants from the National Institutes of Health (grant numbers U01-HG008680 [AAG] and F30-DK116473 [EEG]). Footnotes DISCLOSURE All the authors declared no competing interests. REFERENCES 1. Vivante A, Hildebrandt F. Exploring the genetic basis of early-onset chronic kidney disease. Nat Rev Nephrol. 2016;12: 133–146. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Devuyst O, Knoers NV, Remuzzi G, et al. Rare inherited kidney diseases: challenges, opportunities, and perspectives. Lancet. 2014;383:1844–1859. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Connaughton DM, Kennedy C, Shril S, et al. Monogenic causes of chronic kidney disease in adults. Kidney Int. 2019;95:914–928. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lata S, Marasa M, Li Y, et al. Whole-exome sequencing in adults with chronic kidney disease: a pilot study. Ann Intern Med. 2018;168:100–109. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Rasouly HM, Groopman EE, Heyman-Kantor R, et al. The burden of candidate pathogenic variants for kidney and genitourinary disorders emerging from exome sequencing. Ann Intern Med. 2018. 10.7326/M18-1241. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 6. Malone AF, Phelan PJ, Hall G, et al. Rare hereditary COL4A3/COL4A4 variants may be mistaken for familial focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Kidney Int. 2014;86:1253–1259. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Posey JE, Harel T, Liu P, et al. Resolution of disease phenotypes resulting from multilocus genomic variation. N Engl J Med. 2017;376: 21–31. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Mallett AJ, McCarthy HJ, Ho G, et al. Massively parallel sequencing and targeted exomes in familial kidney disease can diagnose underlying genetic disorders. Kidney Int. 2017;92:1493–1506. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. O’Daniel JM, McLaughlin HM, Amendola LM, et al. A survey of current practices for genomic sequencing test interpretation and reporting processes in US laboratories. Genet Med. 2017;19:575–582. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',173,'2025-10-15 06:42:11','2025-10-15 06:42:11');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3449,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9378635','Clin-Star corner: What is new at the interface of geriatrics and nephrology?','Nair D, Hall RK.','2022','cockcroft gault overestimations nephrotoxins egfrs albuminuric dapa albuminuric albuminuric diuresis natriuresis cric albuminuric liberalize manjula kurella k12hs026395 k76ag059930 k12hs026395 devika k76ag059930 rasheeda rasheeda interpreation conflcits kidneydisease inrig califf tasneem peixoto w42 w47 giannelli windham pizzarelli guralnik underascertainment nutt eneanya multiplier eneanya reese reconsidering duggal montez rath kurella inker eneanya kdoqi gfr_calculator baweja reassessing gudnason celis hunsicker rohde dcct ashurst varagunam raftery yaqoob slows perkovic jardine stefansson rotter aztrazeneca farxiga farxiga tsimihodimos filippatos elisaf halter hazzard cahn mosenzon wiviott lambourg colvin doerfler cric balkrishnan hirth e2016839','Abstract Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is prevalent and burdensome among older adults in the United States. CKD affects at least 15% of the US population, and adults over 65 comprise the largest subset within this group. In this special article, we highlight key findings of three recent original investigations in nephrology and describe each study, relevance to the care of older adults, and current areas of uncertainty that warrant further investigation. Articles relate to removal of the race adjustment in the estimation of kidney function, the use of novel therapeutics to halt CKD progression and improve cardiovascular outcomes, and medication management for short-term pain control in CKD. Keywords: aging, chronic kidney disease, nephrology INTRODUCTION Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a growing public health concern. Over 15% of the US population has CKD. One in three adults with diabetes and one in five adults with hypertension may have CKD, and Medicare costs attributed to CKD amount to over $81 billion. 1 At least 38% of adults aged 65 and older have moderate to advanced CKD, as compared to 12% of those aged 45–64. 2 In 2018, adjusted mortality was more than twice as high among Medicare beneficiaries aged 66 years or older with CKD as compared to those without CKD. 2 Given this growing burden of CKD in older adults, we set out to highlight recent evidence that not only impacts the practice of nephrology, but also has implications for other clinicians who care for older adults with CKD. Herein, we provide critical analysis and interpretation of each of these investigations, describe clinical implications, and discuss areas of uncertainty and need for further, rigorous research. METHODS We set out to identify important studies in nephrology that would be broadly applicable for the management of older adults with CKD. As practicing nephrologists with research interests in geriatrics and gerontology, we applied this lens to the process of identifying three topics to highlight in this manuscript. Given the dearth of clinical trials in nephrology, 3 our process started with recognition of topics that were frequently discussed at scientific meetings, in social media, and in prominent nephrology and general medical journals. As of August 2021, two topic areas that were frequently discussed were clinical trials of sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (SGLT2is) and observational studies demonstrating the need to abandon the inclusion of race in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) estimating equations were timely and increasingly being discussed. We identified one study in each of these topic areas to highlight in this manuscript. The studies were selected because study populations included older adults and findings demonstrated clinical relevance to older adults with CKD. We then sought to identify a third topic through a general literature review from January 2019 to August 2021. Our aim was to identify studies that may not have been typically identified in geriatrics or gerontology journals but which were still relevant to the management of older adults with CKD. We searched general medical journals with high-impact factors (New England Journal of Medicine, the Journal of the American Medical Association journals, Lancet) as well as prominent journals in nephrology (the American Journal of Kidney Diseases, the American Society of Nephrology [ASN] journals). We specifically examined the “Best of ASN Journals” lists compiled in 2019 and 2020, a collection of high impact articles identified by the editorial teams of each journal. This search yielded manuscripts which we each reviewed independently for novelty and timeliness. We narrowed this list to one study based on its relevance to management of older adults with CKD. Informed by the methodological approach from the annual Update in Nephrology and Hypertension previously published in the Annals of Internal Medicine, 4 we provide background information on each article''s topic area, a critical appraisal of its strengths and limitations, its implications for clinical practice, and relevant areas of uncertainty and need for further investigation, as indicated. Removal of the race coefficient from estimating equations for kidney function Background Historically, assessment of kidney function for older adults has been challenging. Inaccurate measures that overestimate or underestimate kidney function can yield delayed nephrology care or unnecessary nephrology referrals, respectively. Use of Creatinine (Cr) alone can cause clinicians to miss CKD diagnoses. 5 Cr-based estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) equations (Cockcroft-Gault, Modification of Diet in Renal Disease, Chronic Kidney Disease-Epidemiology Collaboration-Cr, Berlin Initiative Study) improved kidney function assessment by including additional variables (e.g., age, weight, gender, race). 6 However, as serum creatinine (Cr) alone can be affected by muscle mass (causing overestimations in kidney function for older adults with reduced muscle mass) and medications, these eGFR equations have limited accuracy. For black older adults, there is potential for not only age, but also race to affect accurate estimation of kidney function. The inclusion of race in eGFR equations can result in the overestimation of kidney function, particularly for patients with Stage 3 or Stage 4 CKD. 7 As a result, black older adults may not receive CKD care or kidney transplantation referrals until late in their disease course and risk inaccurate medication dosing or exposure to nephrotoxins which can further harm kidney function. Because race is a social construct, its inclusion in equations to estimate glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) is now recognized as inappropriate. 8 An observational study using data from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) and the Veterans Affairs (VA) Health Care System examined changes in CKD prevalence and implications for clinical care after race was removed from the 2009 CKD-EPI eGFR equations. 9 Key findings After removing the race coefficient, CKD prevalence among black adults increased from 5.2% to 10.6% in NHANES and from 12.4% to 21.6% in the VA cohort. Subgroup analyses among adults aged >60 years demonstrated increased CKD prevalence in both cohorts, particularly at eGFR ranges of 30–59 ml/min/1.73m 2 . Among black veterans, 41.0% of gabapentin users, 33.5% of ciprofloxacin users, and 24.0% of metformin users were reclassified to a lower level of kidney function for which dose reduction or discontinuation is recommended. Strengths and limitations This study''s strength lies in its use of VA data, the largest health care system in the United States. At least 50% of the cohort represented adults over age 60, and nearly 18% were black race. However, limitations include: 1) the use of a single eGFR measure to estimate prevalence of CKD, 2) underrepresentation of women in the dataset, and 3) the study did not include medication doses to allow more detailed evaluation of medication adjustments that would occur with removal of the race coefficient. Clinical implications for geriatricians A new CKD-EPI eGFR equation without a race coefficient has been developed. 10 Geriatricians should adopt this equation, available on the National Kidney Foundation website, in clinical practice as laboratories may continue to report eGFR with a race coefficient. 11 , 12 Furthermore, given the influence of muscle mass on serum creatinine, geriatricians should consider estimating GFR with this new equation using both creatinine and cystatin C (or cystatin C alone) for clinical decisions such as medication dosing, that rely on more accurate estimates of kidney function. 10 , 13 Areas of uncertainty and need for further investigation While development and validation cohorts of the 2021 CKD-EPI eGFR equation had representation among adults aged >65 years and adults with diabetes, there remains limited evidence on the performance of this new eGFR equation among adults with severe multimorbidity. 10 Also, the new creatinine eGFR equations can yield a higher prevalence of CKD among black older adults. 10 Given this has implications for population health, it raises the concern for how to manage older adults with reduced eGFR. With ongoing consideration for age-adapted eGFR thresholds to distinguish older adults with age-related eGFR decline from those with a CKD diagnosis, 14 there remains a need for additional studies to confirm such thresholds do not cause misclassification of patients with moderately reduced eGFRs who can have high risk of cardiovascular diseases. 15 DAPAGLIFLOZIN IN CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE Background Until recently, pharmacological strategies to slow kidney disease progression have mostly focused on angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers to reduce blood pressure and albuminuria, antihyperglycemic agents to optimize blood glucose control, and bicarbonate supplementation in advanced CKD. 16 - 18 Recent randomized controlled clinical trials, however, have demonstrated that SGLT2is are a new therapeutic option to slow kidney disease progression and improve cardiovascular outcomes for adults with Type 2 diabetes mellitus and albuminuric CKD. 19 To test whether SGLT2is would have similar effects in non-diabetic adults with CKD, the Dapagliflozin in Patients with Chronic Kidney Disease (DAPA-CKD) trial tested whether 10 mg of daily oral dapagliflozin versus placebo reduced the risk of adverse kidney and cardiovascular outcomes in 4304 non-diabetic adults (median age 62) with albuminuric CKD (urine albumin creatinine ratio between 200 and 5000 mg/g) and an eGFR of 25–75 ml/min/1.73 m 2 . 20 Key findings 9.2% of adults receiving dapagliflozin versus 14.5% of patients receiving placebo achieved the primary outcome (composite of decline in eGFR >50%, development of kidney failure, or death from kidney or cardiovascular causes) over a median follow-up of 2.4 years (hazard ratio [HR] 0.61; 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.51–0.72). Strengths and limitations Strengths of this study included its minimization of bias by using a randomized controlled intention-to-treat design, meaning that participants were analyzed as originally randomized; its large, international enrollment size (450 study sites over 20 countries); and its appropriate length of follow-up needed to achieve the primary outcome (mean 2.4 years). Relevant to the care of older adults, the trial mostly included those aged >60 years (mean age 62) with advanced CKD (mean eGFR 43 ml/min/1.73 m 2 ) and multimorbidity, highly representative of the populations that nephrologists and geriatricians care for. The study''s limitations include that the study''s inclusion criteria limit generalizability: participants were required to be taking the maximum dose of an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker for at least 4 weeks prior to randomization. Patients with Type 1 diabetes mellitus, hepatic impairment, and vasculitis were excluded, so the findings are not applicable to these specific patient groups. Another limitation is that the trial was stopped early due to data showing significant improvements in the primary outcome. As a result, the trial may have been underpowered to detect other patient-centered endpoints such as glycated hemoglobin level and hypoglycemia. Last, no data are available regarding participants'' functional status so older adults with limited functional status may have been underrepresented. Clinical implications for geriatricians In April 2021, the Food and Drug Administration approved dapagliflozin for adults with albuminuric CKD regardless of diabetes status. It also granted dapagliflozin a “Breakthrough Therapy Designation” for chronic kidney disease, designed to accelerate the development and regulatory review of medications that fulfill an unmet need in chronic disease care. 21 , 22 SGLT2is have pleiotropic effects that move beyond what is achievable by ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers alone. Evidence supports their reductions in body weight and obesity; their diuresis, natriuresis, and improvements in renal oxygenation; and their reductions in reactive oxygen species, inflammation, and vascular proliferation in the kidney. 23 , 24 Given these cardioprotective mechanisms and the high risk of cardiovascular mortality among older adults with CKD, 25 geriatricians should partner with nephrologists to discuss timely use of dapagliflozin and other SGLT2is for their patients with CKD and albuminuria. Notably, a post-hoc safety and efficacy analysis of dapagliflozin in older adults demonstrated similar rates of fractures, volume depletion, malignancy, cystitis, and amputations in both treatment and placebo arms. 26 Areas of uncertainty and need for further investigation Future studies of SGLT2is should include those with diverse etiologies of kidney disease, those with minimal to no albuminuria, and the very elderly, including those with limited functional status, such that more evidence is available to weigh the risks and benefits of this medication class for shared decision-making. NON-STEROIDAL ANTI-INFLAMMATORY AGENTS VERSUS OPIOIDS FOR SHORT-TERM PAIN CONTROL IN CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE Background Pain is increasingly being recognized as patient-prioritized and prevalent in individuals living with kidney disease. A recent systematic review and meta-analysis of 116 studies among 40,678 individuals with kidney disease reported 60% prevalence rate of pain, and meta-regression analyses demonstrated that an older mean age was associated with a higher prevalence of pain perception. 27 Unfortunately, older adults with CKD have limited therapeutic options for pain management due to both age and CKD. The Beers Criteria suggest avoidance of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents (NSAIDS) in stages 4–5 CKD and reducing dose of other medications used for pain (i.e., such as gabapentin, tramadol), and caution use of opioids for chronic pain. 28 To better understand the risk of harm associated with opioids or NSAIDs for short-term pain control in CKD, a recent observational prospective cohort study of 3939 adults (age 21–74) with eGFR 20–70 ml/min/1.73 m 2 evaluated the association of patient-reported use of opioids or NSAIDS over a 30-day recall period with CKD progression, kidney failure, hospitalization, or mortality. 29 Key findings Among adults with CKD aged ≥65 years, 28% and 24% used opioids and NSAIDS, respectively. At a median follow-up of 6.84 years, opioid use was associated with the following outcomes: a composite of 50% eGFR reduction and need for dialysis (HR 1.4; 95% CI 1.2–1.7), need for dialysis alone (HR 1.4; 95% CI 1.1–1.7), death (HR 1.5; 95% CI 1.2–2.0), and hospitalization rate (rate ratio [RR] 1.7; 95% CI 1.6–1.9). However, NSAID use was only associated with increased risk for a composite of 50% eGFR reduction and need for dialysis (HR 1.2; 95% CI 1.0–1.5) as well as hospitalization rate (RR 1.1; 95% CI 1.0–1.3). Strengths and limitations Strengths of this study lie in its novel clinical question and its leveraging of the Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort (CRIC), a large prospective cohort of individuals with advanced CKD containing participants from seven US centers and 13 clinical sites. Though a 30-day recall period was used to identify patients'' frequency of NSAID use, participants were asked to maintain a list of medications or bring the actual medications to study visits. Thoughtful time-dependent covariates were included in the analytical plan, including frequency of depressive symptom severity, quality of life, presence of comorbidities associated with pain such as arthritis and malignancy, and the inclusion of patients'' use of non-opioid/non-NSAID analgesics such as tramadol. Limitations include the non-randomized study design, the use of self-report to identify analgesic use, the lack of detailed information regarding individuals'' degree of pain severity, the lack of data on participant comorbidities related to increased cardiovascular or bleeding risk, and the possibility of confounding by indication, whereby use of an analgesic may have been driven by factors associated with the outcome rather than the exposure. Clinical implications for geriatricians While this is an observational study with key limitations as described above, these findings add to the nascent evidence base that NSAIDs may be considered for short-term pain relief for older, multimorbid adults with CKD for whom opioids may be dangerous or contraindicated. 30 However, clinicians should consider each patient''s risk for cardiovascular or bleeding complications before prescribing or recommending short-term NSAID use. Areas of uncertainty and need for further investigation No randomized controlled trials testing either short-term or long-term pain management strategies in CKD exist so the quality of evidence guiding clinical decisions is limited. Far more research is needed to identify the precise mechanisms, determinants, and pathways of pain and pain perception in both older age and CKD. Furthermore, novel non-pharmacologic pain management strategies need to be developed for those patients for whom both opioids and NSAIDs carry risks of harm (e.g., advanced liver disease, older adults with CKD). CONCLUSION Each of these investigations and the subsequent research that follows will impact clinical care in nephrology, as well as, provide important clinical implications for geriatricians as well. Geriatricians should note that new equations to estimate GFR no longer include a race adjustment and should also consider using cystatin-c-based estimates of GFR that are less affected by muscle mass. Geriatricians should consider prescribing SGLT2is to older adults with albuminuric CKD regardless of diabetes status to delay the onset of kidney failure and optimize cardiovascular outcomes. However, such clinical decisions should take into consideration a patient''s care preferences and their comorbidity burden as it relates to the side effect profile of SGLT2is. Last, if additional high quality studies demonstrate adverse sequelae of opioids in CKD, geriatricians and nephrologists should liberalize the use of NSAIDs for short-term pain control after taking into consideration a patients'' individual cardiovascular and bleeding risk. Key points Geriatricians should adopt use of the new equations, which do not include a race coefficient, to estimate glomerular filtration rate. Sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitors show promise in slowing kidney disease progression in older adults with chronic kidney disease irrespective of diabetes status. Compared to opioids, Non-st*** may carry lower risk of harm for short-term pain control in patients with chronic kidney disease. Why does this paper matter? We identified recent investigations in nephrology that have clinical implications for geriatricians who care for older adults with chronic kidney disease. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors would like to acknowledge the following for review of the scientific content of this manuscript: Manjula Kurella Tamura, MD, MPH, Professor of Medicine, Division of Nephrology, Department of Medicine, Stanford University Medical Center. Funding information Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality, Grant/Award Number: K12HS026395; American Society of Nephrology Foundation for Kidney Research; National Institute on Aging, Grant/Award Number: K76AG059930 FINANCIAL DISCLOSURE K12HS026395 to Devika Nair. K76AG059930 to Rasheeda K. Hall. American Society of Nephrology Foundation for Kidney Research to Rasheeda K. Hall. SPONSOR''S ROLE The authors'' funding sources did not participate in design, approach, interpreation of data or preparation of the paper. Footnotes CONFLICT OF INTEREST The authors have no conflcits. REFERENCES 1. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Chronic Kidney Disease in the United States. 2021. Accessed November 21, 2021. https://www.cdc.gov/kidneydisease/publications-resources/ckd-national-facts.html . 2. National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases. Kidney Disease Statistics for the United States: Fast Facts on Kidney Disease. Accessed November 30, 2021. https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/health-statistics/kidney-disease . 3. Inrig JK, Califf RM, Tasneem A, et al. The landscape of clinical trials in nephrology: a systematic review of Clinicaltrials.gov . Am J Kidney Dis. 2014;63(5):771–780. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Peixoto AJ. Update in nephrology and hypertension: evidence published in 2015. Ann Intern Med. 2016;164(9):W42–W47. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Giannelli SV, Patel KV, Windham BG, Pizzarelli F, Ferrucci L, Guralnik JM. Magnitude of underascertainment of impaired kidney function in older adults with normal serum creatinine. J Am Geriatr Soc. 2007;55(6):816–823. doi: 10.1111/j.1532-5415.2007.01196.x [ DOI ] [ PMC free arti