INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3486,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12124192','Leveraging the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research to Develop the American College of Rheumatology''s Toolkit for Implementation of Rheumatoid Arthritis Outcome Measures in Clinical Practice: A Qualitative Study.','Nasrallah C, Schmajuk G, Hamblin A, Wilson C, Kersey E, Young C, Katz P, Bajaj P, Downey C, Bartels C, Zell J, Danila MI, Ferguson S, Barton JL, DeQuattro K, Yazdany J.','2024','qcdr amcs qcdr permissions amcs amcs ratoolkit kotobee amcs nextgen amazing athena cerner emds medisoft unicharts centricity cprs allscripts sdai mdhaq sdai mdhaq dimentional rapid3 mdhaq icd9 mdhaq rapid3 mdhaq ipad nextgen docked tsi nextgen tsi nextgen tsi hqm clipboard lobby q14 customizing sends ipad ipad ipads ipad ipads ipads hated tough reevaluate reprioritize tsi flowsheets docked uploading nextgen flowsheets authorizations q14 amcs downloadable downloadable succeeding amcs nihms2074056 appendix_a nihms2074056 appendix_b nihms2074056 supplementary_material_1 nihms2074056 supplementary_material_2 ratoolkit kotobee graciously rephrased r18 hs025638 k24ar074534 niams helmick felson bessette haraoui rampakakis dembowy trepanier schipper vermeer kuper ramiro biodam bacalao butow dhillon sundaresan fautrel piette centricity harle listhaus covarrubias klika piuzzi e070 gensheimer eugs eugw bansback clowse tonner schmajuk subash dequattro huberman saldana sourcebook damschroder aron kirsh lowery crosson cromp bulsara belongingness keddem agha s59 s69 pitchforth vallance mossialos millner fistein misconceiving schmajuk myslinski humbert droz izadi schmajuk hammam izadi e31186 laitio giunti halonen e40875 breidenbach wesselmann sibert nordan blanchfield niazi spaulding kash gamm haverman absolom taliercio brundage bantug prodpsa nihms2074056 appendix_a nihms2074056 appendix_b nihms2074056 supplementary_material_1 nihms2074056 supplementary_material_2','Abstract Objective: Despite the recognized benefits of collecting rheumatoid arthritis (RA) outcomes measures, their use in routine care is inconsistent. Using the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR), we conducted semi-structured interviews with United States rheumatologists and practice personnel to assess workflows, opportunities, and challenges in collecting RA outcome measures. Using insights from interviews, we developed the RA Measures Toolkit to enhance their utilization in clinical practice. Methods: We invited 138 RISE registry practices and 5 academic medical centers with ≥ 30 patients eligible for RA outcome measures to participate in the study. Practices were classified based on their performance in quality payment programs. Recorded interviews were transcribed verbatim and analyzed thematically using deductive and inductive techniques. The findings were used to create the RA Measures Toolkit. Results: We conducted 20 interviews with 38 participants across 20 practices. Key themes within the CFIR domains highlighted the challenges and best practices in RA outcome measure collection and included: 1) Process: the variability in practices’ use of RA outcome measures and the importance of streamlined workflows, 2) Intervention: challenges of integrating PROs into electronic health records (EHRs), and 3) Individual characteristics: importance of clinic culture around quality improvement. Using this data, we developed the RA Toolkit, a multimedia online resource, featuring guidelines, best practices, and educational resources to improve the efficiency of current workflows and to enhance patient care. Conclusion: This study identifies critical gaps in the collection of RA outcome measures in U.S. rheumatology practices and provides actionable recommendations and resources to address challenges via the RA Toolkit. Introduction Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is the most common inflammatory arthritis, affecting up to 1% of United States (U.S.) adults, and causes significant disability, excess mortality, and economic burden. 1 Among chronic diseases, RA has decades of robust scientific evidence supporting the role of patient-reported outcomes (PROs), including disease activity (DA) and functional status (FS) measures, to predict outcomes and inform clinical care. 2 – 5 Collecting outcome measures during routine clinical encounters enhances patient-clinician communication, facilitates meaningful discussions regarding treatment options, and increases the likelihood of achieving remission. 6 , 7 Currently, about half of U.S. rheumatology practices consistently gather RA outcome measures. 8 Evidence suggests that healthcare teams often lack the resources and knowledge to effectively collect and use PROs in their clinical decision-making. 6 , 8 – 10 Complex workflows may impede the routine collection of outcome measure data, particularly in the absence of adequate training for medical personnel in administering these assessments and informatics tools to ease electronic health records (EHR) documentation of these measures. Notably, for outcome measures to achieve their intended success, effective system design should ease the burdens on staff, clinicians, and patients. 8 , 11 However, a study on the barriers and facilitators of collecting RA outcome measures, including PROs, in practice has not yet been performed. To address this gap, we investigated the successes and challenges U.S. rheumatology practices face in collecting RA DA and FS measures. We leveraged the data infrastructure of the American College of Rheumatology’s (ACR) Rheumatology Informatics System for Effectiveness (RISE) registry, an EHR-enabled Qualified Clinical Data Registry (QCDR) designed to improve the quality of care for rheumatic conditions. 12 , 13 The registry collects data from the EHRs of participating practices, aggregates and analyzes data centrally, and continuously feeds back performance on quality measures and outcomes to practices via a web-based dashboard. In this study, our aims were: 1) To use RISE data to characterize practices according to performance on quality measures related to the collection of RA outcome measures; 2) To assess RA outcome measures collection and use by rheumatology practice personnel; 3) To develop the RA Measures Toolkit, a comprehensive resource aimed at guiding rheumatology practices in the efficient and effective collection and utilization of RA outcome measures. Materials and Methods RISE Registry We used RISE data to characterize performance on RA outcome measures-based quality measures and select practices for this study. RISE is the ACR’s EHR-enabled registry that automatically extracts data from individual rheumatology practices’ EHRs on a scheduled basis and transfers it to a central data warehouse. 12 RISE includes data from about one-third of U.S. rheumatology practices, mainly community-based and geographically dispersed (rather than academic medical centers (AMCs)). Because of federal QCDR permissions, data on all patients seen by participating rheumatologists is uploaded to the registry, avoiding selection bias. Rheumatologists use RISE for local quality improvement and submission to the Merit-Based Incentive Payment System (MIPS), capturing annual performance on the Quality Payment Program (QPP) quality measures related to RA DA and FS. Selection of practices. Using purposive sampling, we invited RISE practices with ≥ 30 patients eligible for RA outcomes-related quality measures to participate in the study (N=138). We classified practices into 3 categories based on the distribution of their QPP measure for periodic assessment of DA: High-performing practices: “Percentage of RA patients aged ≥18 years who have an assessment of DA using an ACR-preferred RA DA assessment tool at ≥ 50% of encounters for RA during the measurement year” (n=71), middle performing practices: RA DA assessment tool at > 0% to < 50% (n=38) and low performing practices: RA DA assessment tool = 0% (n=29). Because AMCs are under-represented in the RISE registry, we invited 5 AMCs with varying quality measure performance, to participate in a larger learning collaborative (LC) aimed at improving RA care quality. 14 As part of the LC, we assessed these AMC’s collection of RA outcome measures. A recruitment email was sent to rheumatologists and key staff from eligible practices involved in RA measure collection, including information technology (IT) personnel, practice managers, medical assistants (MAs), or nurse practitioners (NPs), inviting them to participate in virtual interviews. Multiple people from the same practice could join the interview. The invitation explained the study’s purpose, voluntary nature, and asked interested individuals to contact the study team. Practices could choose interview participants based on those involved in collecting RA measures. Data Collection Researchers trained in qualitative data collection (CN, CY, JY) conducted virtual interviews via Zoom between January 2022 and 2023. Each interview was conducted by one researcher with a designated primary responder from each practice while the others took notes. The interviews were guided by a semi-structured interview guide, which was developed based on the core concepts of the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) assessing 1) practice resources and characteristics 2) workflows for RA measure collection, 3) challenges and opportunities in collecting RA outcome measures, and 4) suggestions to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of current workflows. The interview guide was iteratively revised by our team, the ACR, and LC rheumatologists, based on pilot testing with team members (JY, GS) and two additional rheumatologists. It was approved by the University of California San Francisco Institutional Review Board ( Appendix A ). Verbal consent was obtained from participants before the interviews. Data saturation 15 was reached before completing all interviews (i.e., later interviews were not yielding new perspectives on challenges and opportunities in collecting RA measures) but we continued data collection to capture diverse practice settings, performance levels, locations, and EHR types. Interviews lasted between 30 to 80 min, were audio recorded, and transcribed verbatim with transcripts checked for accuracy by two researchers (CN, CY). Participants received modest compensation. Conceptual Framework We used the CFIR to guide our study, providing a structured approach to understanding factors influencing RA outcome measure collection and use. The CFIR, widely used in health services research studies, systematically evaluates multilevel implementation, identifying barriers and opportunities that influence intervention implementation and effectiveness and can highlight successful practices across various contexts. 16 – 18 The framework comprises five domains: 1) Implementation process, 2) Intervention characteristics, 3) Individuals characteristics; 4) Inner setting, and 5) Outer Setting. 16 , 17 Data Analysis Responses to open-ended questions were thematically analyzed using deductive and inductive techniques. First, a qualitative researcher (CN) reviewed the data to apply deductive codes based on the CFIR model and created preliminary inductive codes to capture emergent ideas within and across interviews. The team discussed, revised, and structured the codes into a codebook. Two coders (CN, CY) independently applied codes to interviews, reviewed each other’s work, and transformed the results into a matrix to identify key themes. Using an iterative consensus process, we organized emerging themes and sub-themes into the five CFIR domains and identified exemplary quotes illustrating innovations, resources, best practices, and challenges in collecting RA outcome measures. The study adhered to the Consolidated Criteria for reporting Qualitative Research (COREQ) checklist ( Supplementary material 1 ). RA Toolkit Development Due to the lack of national resources for rheumatologists on collecting and using RA measures, we used a consensus-building method 18 – 20 to incorporate feedback and translate the findings from the semi-structured interviews, ACR guidelines regarding RA outcome measures, and other evidence-based resources, into a web-based RA measures Toolkit that is available at ratoolkit.kotobee.com ( Figure 1 ). Best practices and facilitators from interviews, including workflows for different clinical scenarios (e.g., in-person visits with paper or electronic PRO collection workflows, telehealth visits), were summarized and integrated into the Toolkit to ensure it reflects real-world experience. We developed educational and training resources tailored to address the concerns and needs identified by rheumatologists and practice staff during interviews. These resources aim to enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of collecting and utilizing RA outcome measures. To ensure their practicality, we pilot-tested the training materials with one rheumatologist, three MAs, and one practice manager. Additionally, we tested the patient educational resources with around 20 patients at an academic rheumatology clinic in northern California, gathering feedback and incorporating their suggestions. Finally, we included video interviews with rheumatologists in various practice settings who provided insights into the innovative methods they have devised for collecting RA outcome measures. The Toolkit was revised iteratively with input from 14 LC rheumatologists. We also used the Expert Recommendations for Implementing Change (ERIC) checklist as a mapping tool for the evidence-based RA Measures Toolkit ( supplementary material 2 ). Figure 1: Methods for Developing the Rheumatoid Arthritis Outcome Measures Toolkit. Open in a new tab RA: Rheumatoid Arthritis; CFIR: Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research; QPP: Quality Payment Program; EHR: Electronic Health Records; RISE: Rheumatology Informatics System for Effectiveness; MA: Medical Assistant; ACR: American College of Rheumatology Results We conducted 20 interviews across 15 RISE practices and five AMCs with 38 participants, including interprofessional teams involved in implementing and using RA outcome measures, including PROs. Participants included 21 rheumatologists, eight MAs, six practice managers, one NP, and two health IT specialists. Of the 15 RISE practices, seven had high performance on DA measures, five had middle performance and three had low performance. Practice characteristics are summarized in Table 1 and Appendix B . Emerging themes were organized into the five CFIR domains. Table 1: Characteristics of practices interviewed regarding PRO measure collection. Total number of practices N=20 Reported Performance 1 High n (%) 11 (55%) Non-RISE Academic Medical Center 3 (27%) RISE practice 2 8 (73%) Middle 5 (25%) Non-RISE Academic Medical Center 5 (100%) Low 4 (20%) Non-RISE Academic Medical Center 1(25%) RISE practice 3 (75%) Type of practice Single Specialty 8 (40%) Academic Medical Center 5 (25%) Solo practice 6 (30%) Multi-specialty 1 (5%) Geographic Region Northeast 2 (10%) Midwest 3 (15%) Southeast 4 (20%) West 5 (25%) Southwest 6 (30%) EHR vendor E-Clinical Works 4 (20%) Epic 3 (15%) NextGen 5 (25%) Amazing Charts 1 (5%) Athena Health 1 (5%) Cerner 1 (5%) eMDs-Medisoft Clinical 1 (5%) Unicharts 1 (5%) GE Centricity 1 (5%) CPRS 1 (5%) Allscripts professionals 1 (5%) Primary type of insurance accepted Private 16 (80%) Public 14 (70%) Medicare/Medicaid 6 (43%) Medicare only 8 (57%) VA 1 (5%) RA outcome measures collected 3 Disease Activity Measures CDAI 18 (90%) RAPID-3 9 (45%) DAS-28 5 (25%) SDAI 3 (15%) Functional status MDHAQ 6 (30%) HAQ 4 (20%) HAQ-II 2 (10%) Pain score 2 (10%) Patient global only 2 (10%) Open in a new tab 1 RISE practices (n-8): 7 community-based practices and 1 Academic Medical Center 2: Performance: High: Percentage of patients aged 18 years and older with a diagnosis of RA who have an assessment of DA using an ACR-preferred RA assessment tool at ≥ 50% of encounters for RA during the measurement year”; Middle performing practices: RA disease activity assessment tool at > 0% to ≤ 50% and low performing practices: RA disease activity assessment tool ≤ 0% 3 Variables are not mutually exclusive. PRO: Patient Reported Outcome; EHR: Electronic Health Records; RA: Rheumatoid Arthritis; RISE: Rheumatology Informatics System for Effectiveness; VA: Veterans Affairs; DA: Disease Activity; CDAI: Clinical Disease Activity Index; RAPID-3: Routine Assessment of Patient Index Data 3; DAS-28: Disease Activity Score 28 joints; SDAI: Simple Disease Activity Index; MDHAQ: Multi-Dimentional Health Assessment Questionnaire; HAQ: Health Assessment Questionnaire; HAQ-II: Health Assessment Questionnaire II Implementation process Rheumatology practices reported using various RA DA measures, most commonly the Clinical Disease Activity Index (CDAI), which includes tender and swollen joint counts, as well as patient and physician global assessments) (90%); and the Routine Assessment of Patient Index Data 3 (RAPID3, which is a PRO) (55%). The Multi-Dimensional Health Assessment Questionnaire (MDHAQ) (30%) was the most used FS assessment. Practices developed customized workflows suited to their clinic settings and available resources, often tailoring RA outcome measure collection to their EHR software or to optimize success on MIPS metrics. Influenced by the MIPS incentives, these practices focused on data-driven improvement to streamline their workflows and enhance the quality and efficiency of RA care. When asked about strategies used or steps adopted for collecting RA outcome measures, simplicity and efficiency of data collection and entry were the main characteristics described by high-performing practices ( Table 2 , Q1). Table 2: CFIR domains of the generated themes and exemplary quotes from practices with successful collection of RA outcome measures CFIR Domains Themes Quotes (Q) Implementation Process Workflow development Q1 : “I was trying to understand … the simplest way of having the data in a structured field so it will be easily followed over time, and it will always be in the same place” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Patient identification Q2 . “If patient comes and the doctors diagnoses him with RA, put that on the problem list so, then the next time the patient comes in, it’s because there’s a rheumatoid problem on their list with an ICD9 in our EMR we have a built in that if any of these group of codes are on a patient problem list, then they will get a pop up message that says this patient has a rheumatoid arthritis condition and a MDHAQ is needed” (IT Manager, RISE practice) Form Distribution Q3 . “The patients who are web-enabled fill out their questionnaire the night before: what doctors have you seen? Any disease flares? Medicine changes? Lab tests? Procedures that you’ve had done. And RAPID3 (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Checks and balances Q4 . “One of the things I say all the time is “remember to assess your patients and “do your MDHAQ” …Some MAs were like “I put in the score” But they didn’t put in the interpretation, and you have to do both…So I have to remind them” (Practice manager, RISE practice) Scoring Q5 . “The iPad is able to calculate that automatically. Because it has formula in the background” (Rheumatologist, AMC) Intervention Characteristics Transmission to EHR Q6 . “When we went to NextGen, we started using a magic pen. It would take a picture of the RAPID-3, be docked on the computer and by the time I came into the room and opened the patient’s chart, that data was already in there.” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Telehealth visits Q7. “Our MAs will basically check in the patient for their visit, right before the visit. They’ll do the same process in theory that they’re doing with every other in-person patient. Theoretically it should be done the same.” (Practice Manager, AMC) Data validation Q8 . “TSI is what services NextGen for our office. So, it’s TSI/NextGen. And TSI has… Part of the package is a MIPS program. They help us look at the performance metrics, and they have their own reporting structure called HQM. So, practices can choose to use their structure or use the RISE. we’re doing a hybrid, both at the same time. We’ll see which scores are better and then decide which one.” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Individual characteristics Clinic culture around quality improvement Q9 . “Once they come into the clinic and we see that they have not completed those forms, the front desk is also involved…They provide the patients with those forms and a clipboard to complete while they are waiting the lobby until they are brought back” (Medical Assistant, AMC) Training staff Q10 . ““We get them [the staff] trained so they’re used to knowing different ways to get patients to do it.” (Practice Manager, RISE practice) Inner setting Feedback Q11. “We chose RA outcomes as our ongoing performance professional evaluation metric from looking at a list of all the approved metrics as a division and set our own internal goals towards improving the quality of those measures. So, we have that discussion at division meetings, and then we review the data monthly. All the faculty and advanced practice providers are involved and they either give feedback verbally or send emails with questions, concerns, or ideas” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice). Monitoring and evaluation Q12 . “I check on the RISE [registry] every month to see where we are on our measures. If there is any problem… “we should be close to 100% on this. Who’s not doing something?” and then that’s usually when we will bring it up to them” (Practice manager, RISE practice) Resources Q13 . “The way we created our form is they can’t move forward without filling out the required field and so that’s how you know how we tried to prevent some of this stuff” (Practice Manager, RISE practice) Outer setting Regulations Q14 . “MIPS is number one, but I know that providers, even pre-MIPS … use the CDAI and the HAQ form, so they do like to measure how the patients are doing so that they … see their progress” (Practice Manager, RISE practice) Open in a new tab Almost all practices (n=18) reported that the collection of RA outcome measures began with a staff member providing paper forms to the patient upon arrival at the clinic. Few practices (n=9) reported customizing their EHR to flag RA appointments so that front desk staff could selectively administer forms; instead, most administered PRO surveys to all patients regardless of diagnosis ( Table 2 , Q2). Less commonly, six practices administered forms electronically, either through 1) an online patient portal, where the EHR identifies RA patients and automatically sends them texts and/or email reminders ahead of time or 2) a device provided by the clinic, such as an iPad or tablet ( Table 2 , Q1). When asked about characteristics of their workflow that facilitated success in the collection of RA outcome measures, checks and balances were reported by almost all high performing practices including: 1) well-defined roles for practice personnel, 2) standardized procedures for patients to fill out PROs and for clinicians to complete composite measures like CDAI that have both clinician and patient-reported components, and 3) regular training for clinician and staff support for collecting RA outcome measures ( Table 2 , Q4). Most often, clinic staff were responsible for scoring the PRO components of the RA outcome measures and manually entering the scores into the EHR. A few practices discussed automatic calculation of the scores, especially when using mobile tools such as iPad or patient portal questionnaires or having structured fields for RA outcome measures in their EHR, which could reduce data entry burden for staff ( Table 2 , Q5). Technical difficulties, such as cumbersome EHR workflows to enter RA outcomes into structured fields, were major challenges faced by most practices (n=13). Despite utilizing technology such as iPads for collecting and scoring RA outcome measures, one practice reported that data from the iPad did not upload automatically into the EHR. As a result, the practice continued to rely on MAs, nurses, or clinicians to manually extract scored measures from the iPads and input them into the EHR ( Table 3 , Q1). Another practice used the EHR patient portal for data collection, but not all patients used their portal regularly, especially non-English speaking and older patients, necessitating that some surveys continue to be administered by paper ( Table 3 , Q2). Table 3: CFIR domains of the challenges reported by practices with exemplary quotes. CFIR Domains Themes Quotes Implementation Process Technical difficulties Q1 . “We had a time when our program was down, the one that sent the questions to the iPads and so for a week we had to have people do it on paper and the MA hated that because that was more work they had to do in the EMR… so that made it really tough on them so they realized how great technology really was at that point.” (IT Manager, RISE practice) Patient intake Q2 . “[patients] just find the paperwork redundant and a lot of them don’t like to go online” (Medical Assistant, RISE practice) Intervention Characteristics EHR limitations Q3 . “The EMR does not have the ability to capture the structured data… So, I will input those data manually so that my EMR can capture it for the MIPS reporting and the rise registry can catch it.” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Vendor support Q4 . “It was a lot of customization when we initially built it. I think EPIC came out with the joint exam module. But even that required customization to our center… we have somehow created [a workflow] that you have to do these [PROs] before you check in to telehealth.” (Rheumatologist, AMC) Individual characteristics Survey fatigue Q5 . “But it’s really the second or third visit that it becomes a problem because they say, “Oh, I’ve already done that” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Shortage of staff Q6 . “As far as staffing issues, I think it is more of the timing of rooming and kind of re-teaching the medical assistant … how to reevaluate and reprioritize tasks.” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Inner setting Financial limitations Q7 . “I looked into a different EMR, I think the TSI, they have a pretty good incorporation of these data, but the cost is prohibitive, and then the switching EMR as a painful process” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Outer setting RISE dashboard Q8 . “We had a lot of issues communicating with [the registry vendor] last year and they were not extracting data and they were not communicating with my software people effectively to kind of figure out the problems that they were having on getting the data.” (Rheumatologist, RISE practice) Open in a new tab Intervention Characteristics Data transmission of RA outcome measures to the EHR varied between practices. More than half of respondents mentioned that the data was entered manually into nursing flowsheets by the medical support staff or the clinician. Electronic transmission of data into the EHR was reported by a few participants through an app, online portal, electronic data collection form, or scanned after being physically filled out by the patient on paper. One practice reported using a “magic pen”, a device allowing patients to complete paper PRO forms, which automatically scans their responses and can be docked for uploading into the EHR ( Table 2 , Q6). While almost all practices had some virtual visits with patients, very few reported successful collections of PROs during telehealth encounters. One practice noted requiring patients to schedule an in-person encounter to collect the RA outcome measures ( Table 2 , Q7). Concerning the monitoring and maintenance of RA outcome measure collection workflow, practices reported that success was often driven by either a central clinic champion in smaller practices or by federal quality reporting needs of the clinic overall in larger practices ( Table 2 , Q8). In terms of challenges with RA outcome measures collection, lack of automatic score calculation and inability of the EHR to capture structured data from scanned forms and uploaded pdf files were challenges faced by some practices when collecting measures and transmitting them electronically to the RISE registry ( Table 3 , Q3). Almost all study participants expressed frustration over inadequate EHR vendor support for collecting RA outcome measures; a few EHRs in the sample (NextGen and Epic) had software to facilitate entry and longitudinal tracking of data specifically for RA. Respondents reported that even larger vendors such as Epic required some degree of local customization for RA outcome measures entry and tracking ( Table 3 , Q4). Individual Characteristics Nearly all interview participants emphasized the importance of clinic culture around quality improvement for the success of their current RA outcome measure collection workflows ( Table 2 , Q9). Clinicians and medical staff both reported the importance of consistency and proper training in the methods and reasons behind the collection of RA measures, and many emphasized the importance of educating patients about the importance of collecting PROs and how they would be used ( Table 2 , Q10). Despite this recognition, most practices reported that patient survey fatigue was a key barrier faced when collecting RA outcome measures. In fact, some participants reported that patients would fill out the PRO measures at their first visit but then decline the same questionnaires at the second and third visit, which limited their ability to collect RA outcome measures longitudinally ( Table 3 , Q5). Other practices highlighted the shortage of medical support staff or the limited training of MAs in efficiently collecting, scoring, and entering RA measures as significant individual-level challenges ( Table 3 , Q6). Inner Setting The capacity for continuous quality improvement of workflows was common for practices with high rates of RA outcome measure collection and use, with many reporting the ability to respond to ad hoc internal feedback on workflow processes and suggestions for improvement ( Table 2 , Q11). Other practices relied on continuous monitoring and evaluation of their performance on RA quality measures via the RISE registry to provide feedback and recommendations to medical staff and clinicians ( Table 2 , Q12). Often clinic leaders reported proactively allocating financial resources, health IT support, and other resources to improve workflows including a rheumatology specific EHR system with customized flowsheets for RA outcome measures, hiring dedicated staff to help with data entry, or facilitating development of customized electronic forms with required fields to aid data collection ( Table 2 , Q13). Conversely, many practices stated that their EHR software did not support documenting RA measures in structured fields. One practice mentioned that upgrading or switching to a new EHR system was needed, but not financially viable ( Table 3 , Q7). Outer Setting The decision to collect RA outcome measures was influenced by ongoing internal and external quality improvement programs, professional opinions on the value of RA outcome measures, federal performance measure reporting, and requirements set by insurance companies for medication prior authorizations ( Table 2 , Q14). However, several practices described data capture problems in the RISE registry dashboard that displays RA outcome measures performance for national quality reporting. Often, this reflected the inability of the registry to capture measures recorded as free text in clinical notes, leading to falsely low-quality performance scores. Many of the interviewed practices who appeared to have low performance based on their RISE data, had standardized and consistent workflows for collecting RA outcome measures ( Table 3 , Q8). Toolkit Development Results of the CFIR-guided interviews conducted with RISE practices and AMCs identifying barriers and facilitators to RA outcome measures collection and use, ACR guidelines regarding RA outcome measures, and other evidence-based resources were compiled to design the RA measures Toolkit, then revised with the LC. The Toolkit is the first comprehensive, evidence-based multimedia online resource for RA outcome measure implementation for clinicians and staff. It supports rheumatology practices in developing efficient, patient-focused workflows for collecting RA outcome measures. The Toolkit contains all ACR recommended DA and FS measures with downloadable forms that aid in understanding RA outcome measures and scoring instructions. Reflecting themes from qualitative interviews, the Toolkit offers recommendations and best practices from high-performing practices across diverse practice settings and EHRs featuring workflow diagrams and case studies videos highlighting the patient-centered approaches that these practices use. The Toolkit also addresses challenges in collecting of RA outcome measures by including validated translations of RA measures in Spanish and Chinese as well as considerations for collecting these measures in diverse populations. We also developed a downloadable staff training guide to facilitate the effective collection of RA measures in practice, and a patient education guide to enhance patients’ knowledge and awareness of the importance of these measures. To further enhance practice workflows, the Toolkit incorporates tips for succeeding in the QPP and guidance on utilizing the RISE registry for tracking performance on RA outcome measures. The Toolkit was launched on the ACR website in August 2023. Discussion In this study, we used the CFIR framework to guide stakeholder interviews and assess the facilitators and barriers to collection and use of RA outcome measures in the clinical setting. While CFIR does not prioritize domains or provide specific solutions, particularly in its “outer setting” domain, it offers a robust framework for identifying a wide range of influences on outcome measure collection and use in RA care. Key findings were the variability in practices’ use of RA outcome measures, the importance of streamlined workflows, and the challenges of integrating PROs into EHRs. Results of the qualitative interviews combined with ACR guidelines and other evidence-based resources regarding RA outcome measures resulted in the development of the RA Measures Toolkit. It is the first multimedia online resource designed to assist clinicians and staff in maximizing the efficiency and effectiveness of RA outcome measure collection and utilization. In our study, rheumatologists felt strongly that RA outcome measures were important for providing high-quality care in RA. Clearly defined workflows combined with a culture of accountability mandating checks and balances and reinforcing the collection of RA outcome measures was a feature of all high-performing practices. In addition, incorporating RA outcome measures capability into the existing EHR made it easy for rheumatologists and medical staff to record these measures and track changes over time. Moreover, capacity of the clinic for rapid adjustments to workflows helped high-performing practices to update and implement changes quickly to improve the collection of RA outcome measures. Interestingly, we found that even practices that appeared to have low performance on measures in RISE were collecting RA outcome measures at least some of the time. It is noteworthy that a considerable number of rheumatologists tend to document RA outcome results in unstructured clinical notes which may not be captured by RISE registry queries. The widespread use of RA outcome measures by rheumatologists, even among low performing practices because of lack of structured data capture, likely reflects rheumatology’s long history of using these measures in clinical care, and the consistent exposure to their collection and use in rheumatology training. The Health Assessment Questionnaire (HAQ) was developed in 1980, 21 and much work has been done to validate related RA outcome measures. This groundwork facilitated achieving national consensus on which RA outcome measures to use in practice, 22 the development of the ACR’s RA quality measures, which include RA outcome measures collection, 23 , 24 and the introduction of these measures into the RISE registry. 12 This broad national consensus and infrastructure reflect the field’s commitment to supporting the collection of RA outcome measures and likely explains the fact that all practices we interviewed were collecting these measures. Our findings also suggest that some rheumatologists have falsely low performance in federal reporting programs because of EHR data capture challenges; technologies like scalable natural language processing of notes to address this challenge are needed. 25 Despite high motivation to collect RA outcome measures and national quality measures endorsing their use, barriers at all levels of the CFIR framework were identified, even among high-performing practices. The main barriers to the implementation of RA outcome measures were related to health IT inner and outer settings. rheumatologists and their staff felt that EHR systems and health-IT infrastructure did not adequately support their efforts to collect and use RA outcome measures in practice. Rheumatologists also mentioned difficulties in having an external system like the RISE registry accurately detect RA outcome data from clinical notes. Thus, advocating for EHR software that supports the integration of RA outcome measure collection, as well as implementation support for staff would be fruitful in further scaling and spreading the use of PROs in rheumatology. 26 Our findings build upon evidence regarding RA outcome measure collection in other fields of medicine. For example, in oncology, studies have shown that the lack of interoperability of EHR systems and the data entry burden for clinical staff are barriers to broader PRO collection. 6 , 27 , 28 In addition, previous work has shown that financial constraints limit investments in EHR software adaptation or other digital applications that more seamlessly integrate PRO collection. 29 , 30 Across different medical fields, studies have shown that regular training and education to build clinical staff capacity to collect PROs can be a challenge. 31 , 32 Similarly, response burden and patient survey fatigue have been significant challenges faced in the collection of PRO measures. 31 – 33 Our research suggests that broader efforts to address the EHR-related, financial and workflow challenges of PRO implementation is needed in healthcare. By analyzing CFIR-guided interview findings, we developed the RA Measures Toolkit to address challenges in implementing standardized RA DA and FS measures. The Toolkit incorporates innovations and opportunities from high-performing practices to help rheumatologists standardize and improve the collection and use of RA outcome measures. Recognizing the value of shared experiences and innovative practices within the rheumatology community, the Toolkit includes best practice examples of workflows from high-performing rheumatology practices across the country to highlight their achievements and patient-centered approaches. In addition, since some practices with low or middle performance on quality measures had incomplete data capture that led to falsely low performance scores, the Toolkit also includes guidance on utilizing the RISE registry to enhance, effectively monitor, and evaluate the implementation of RA measures and to meet the QPP’s requirements and maximize reimbursement. The Toolkit also addresses the needs of practice staff, such as MAs, as well as patients. We found that practices face a variety of individual level challenges hindering the collection and use of RA outcome measures. These include shortage of medical support staff, high staff turnover, patient survey fatigue, and workflow inefficiencies in measure collection. To address these issues, the Toolkit includes a training guide for medical support staff as well as patient education materials on the importance of RA outcome measures. These materials support practices in enhancing the knowledge and awareness of their medical support staff while helping patients understand the relevance and utility of these measures in RA care. Despite its strengths, this study has limitations. Although we aimed to recruit practices with low performance on RA outcome measures based on quantitative data from the RISE registry, many of the practices we interviewed had moderate or higher performance. This discrepancy was secondary to challenges with capturing unstructured EHR data. While we included geographically diverse practices and AMCs and practices, our interviews may not fully represent the wider spectrum of rheumatology practices with different RA outcome workflows or views. Since all interviewed practices collected RA outcome measures, our findings cannot be transferred to those not collecting any measures. Further investigation is needed to confirm the RA Toolkit’s broader applicability and effectiveness, especially for practices not using outcome measures. While the Toolkit addresses key interview themes, some EHR software barriers remain outside its scope and require vendor advocacy by rheumatologists. In conclusion, this study interviewed U.S. rheumatology practices to identify common facilitators and barriers to RA outcome measure collection. Despite most rheumatologists collecting these measures, inefficient workflows, and lack of EHR support, were major barriers. We developed the RA Toolkit to guide effective implementation, training, collection, and use of RA outcome measures to promote quality, value-based patient-centered care. Supplementary Material Appendix A NIHMS2074056-supplement-Appendix_A.docx (23.2KB, docx) Appendix B NIHMS2074056-supplement-Appendix_B.docx (13.3KB, docx) Supplementary material 1 NIHMS2074056-supplement-Supplementary_material_1.docx (19.8KB, docx) Supplementary material 2 NIHMS2074056-supplement-Supplementary_material_2.docx (131.9KB, docx) Significance and Innovations. This is the first study to examine the barriers and facilitators of collecting rheumatoid arthritis (RA) outcome measures, including patient reported outcomes (PROs) in RA practice. We used the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) framework to guide stakeholder qualitative interviews and assess successes and challenges that U.S. rheumatology practices have encountered when collecting RA disease activity and functional status measures. Using a consensus building method to translate the findings from the semi-structured interviews, American College of Rheumatology (ACR) guidelines regarding RA outcome measures, and other evidence-based resources, we developed the web-based RA Outcome Measures Toolkit that is available at ratoolkit.kotobee.com . The ACR RA Toolkit is the first comprehensive, open-source, evidence-based resource available for rheumatologists and healthcare teams to guide effective implementation, training, collection, and use of RA outcome measures that promote quality and value-based patient-centered care. Acknowledgements: We would like to thank many rheumatologists and practice personnel who graciously provided their time to participate in the qualitative interviews presented here and who provided feedback on the RA Toolkit. ChatGPT was used to help shorten the sentences to meet word limits while preserving the original meaning. The generated sentences were then rephrased by the authors to prevent plagiarism. Funding: This project was supported by grant number R18 HS025638 from the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ). The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the AHRQ. Dr. Yazdany is also supported by K24AR074534. Resources related to NIAMS P30 Resource-Based Center for the Advancement of Precision Medicine in Rheumatology were also used in the conduct of this project. References 1. Helmick CG, Felson DT, Lawrence RC, et al. Estimates of the prevalence of arthritis and other rheumatic conditions in the United States. Part I. Arthritis Rheum. 2008;58(1):15–25. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bessette L, Haraoui B, Rampakakis E, Dembowy J, Trepanier MO, Pope J. Effectiveness of a treat-to-target strategy in patients with moderate to severely active rheumatoid arthritis treated with abatacept. Arthritis Res Ther. 2023;25(1):183. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Schipper LG, Vermeer M, Kuper HH, et al. A tight control treatment strategy aiming for remission in early rheumatoid arthritis is more effective than usual care treatment in daily clinical practice: a study of two cohorts in the Dutch Rheumatoid Arthritis Monitoring registry. Ann Rheum Dis. 2012;71(6):845–850. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ramiro S, Landewe RB, van der Heijde D, et al. Is treat-to-target really working in rheumatoid arthritis? a longitudinal analysis of a cohort of patients treated in daily practice (RA BIODAM). Ann Rheum Dis. 2020;79(4):453–459. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Greene GJ, Beaumont JL, Bacalao EJ, et al. Integrating PROMIS Measures in a Treat-to-Target Approach to Standardize Patient-Centered Treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis. J Rheumatol. 2023;50(8):1002–1008. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Nguyen H, Butow P, Dhillon H, Sundaresan P. A review of the barriers to using Patient-Reported Outcomes (PROs) and Patient-Reported Outcome Measures (PROMs) in routine cancer care. J Med Radiat Sci. 2021;68(2):186–195. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scho